    <section class="empire-glance" id="at-a-glance" aria-labelledby="glance-title">
      <div class="empire-glance-main"><h2 class="empire-glance-title" id="glance-title">At a glance</h2><ul><li>The regime grew out of the Nationalist side of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spanish-civil-war">Spanish Civil War</a>: Franco was recognised as Caudillo on 1 October 1936 and in April 1937 merged the Falange and the Carlists into the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fet-y-de-las-jons">FET y de las JONS</a>, the sole legal party.</li><li>Victory in 1939 was followed by mass imprisonment, executions and exile; by the regime's own figures more than 270,000 people were held in prisons and some 500,000 had fled, and more than 200,000 Spaniards are estimated to have died from 1940 to 1942 of persecution, hunger and disease.</li><li>After isolation and autarky, the liberalisation of 1959 opened the &quot;Spanish Miracle&quot; of the 1960s, but political parties and independent unions stayed banned until Franco's death on 20 November 1975 opened the transition to democracy.</li></ul></div>
      <aside class="empire-dyk"><p class="empire-dyk-title">Did you know?</p><p>Under the Law of Political Responsibility of February 1939, parishes were granted policing powers equal to those of local officials and Falange leaders, and some official jobs required a statement of &quot;good behaviour&quot; from a priest.</p></aside>
    </section>

