    <div class="profile-section-block" id="foundation">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Foundation</h2>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The regime emerged from the Nationalist side in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spanish-civil-war">Spanish Civil War</a>. On 1 October 1936 the Junta de Defensa Nacional, which governed the territories the Nationalists held, recognised <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/francisco-franco">Francisco Franco</a> as Caudillo of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a>, the Spanish equivalent of the Duce and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fuhrer">Führer</a>. In April 1937 Franco took control of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/falange-espanola-de-las-jons">Falange Española de las JONS</a>, whose founder José Antonio Primo de Rivera had been executed by the Republican government in November 1936, and merged it with the Carlist Comunión Tradicionalista into the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fet-y-de-las-jons">Falange Española Tradicionalista y de las JONS</a>, the main component of the Movimiento Nacional. The new government was a one-party <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dictatorship">dictatorship</a> explicitly modelled on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fascist-italy">Fascist Italy</a> and Nazi <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a>, with its own Spanish characteristics.</p><p>The Nationalists took the whole of Spain through a methodical war of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/attrition">attrition</a> that involved imprisoning and executing Spaniards found guilty of supporting the values of the Republic: regional autonomy, democracy, free elections and women's rights. The right called these "enemy elements" an "anti-Spain" born of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bolsheviks">Bolsheviks</a> and a "Judeo-Masonic conspiracy". When the war ended in 1939, by the regime's own figures more than 270,000 people were held in prisons and some 500,000 had fled into exile; between six and seven thousand exiles died in Mauthausen.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="socioeconomy">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Socioeconomy</h2>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The Civil War had ravaged the economy, and for more than a decade after it the economy improved little. Franco pursued a fascist-style autarky on the Italian and German models that cut off almost all international trade and aid, and a famine killed over 200,000 people while only black marketeers prospered. In 1940 the Sindicato Vertical became the only legal trade union, under government control. The Instituto Nacional de Colonización, created in 1939, irrigated land and installed settlers in around 300 settlements between 1940 and 1970, while protecting large landowners from expropriation.</p><p>On the brink of bankruptcy, pressure from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a>, which gave about $1.5 billion in aid between 1954 and 1964, from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/international-monetary-fund">IMF</a> and from technocrats of Opus Dei led the regime to liberalise the economy in 1959, over Franco's opposition. Growth took off in the "Spanish Miracle": international firms opened factories, and in the 1960s <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a> was the second-fastest-growing economy in the world after <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/japan">Japan</a>, alongside <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brazil">Brazil</a>. Mass emigration to other European countries relieved surplus population and sent home remittances. Regions such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/madrid">Madrid</a>, Catalonia and the Basque Country fared far better than <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/extremadura">Extremadura</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/galicia">Galicia</a> or <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/andalusia">Andalusia</a>. Divorce, contraception and abortion were forbidden.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="military">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Military</h2>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Franco cut the army from almost one million at the end of the Civil War to 250,000 in early 1940, most of them two-year <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/conscription">conscripts</a>. In November 1942, with the Allied landings in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/north-africa">North Africa</a> and the German occupation of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a>, he ordered a partial mobilisation that brought the army to over 750,000 men; the Air Force and Navy grew to 35,000 airmen and 25,000 sailors by 1945, and the army kept about 400,000 men until the end of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-world-war">Second World War</a>.</p><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a> remained formally non-belligerent or neutral in that war but in practice supported the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/axis-powers">Axis</a>. Franco seriously considered joining for the sake of ideology and of claims to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gibraltar">Gibraltar</a>, French <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/morocco">Morocco</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oran">Oran</a> district of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/algeria">Algeria</a>, and took over the international city of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tangier">Tangier</a> after the defeat of France. The Meeting at Hendaye with Hitler on 23 October 1940 ended in failure over Franco's demands and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a>'s own, and a further attempt in December 1940 for Operation Felix also failed. Spain still sent the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/blue-division">Blue Division</a> to fight on the Eastern Front, let German submarines use its ports and sent tungsten and foodstuffs to Germany. In <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/africa">Africa</a> it gave up its <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/protectorate">protectorate</a> in Morocco in 1956 and granted Equatorial Guinea independence in 1968.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="governance">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Governance &amp; Law</h2>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Franco ruled with, on paper, more power than any Spanish leader before or since; Stanley G. Payne judged that the absence of even a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rubber">rubber</a>-stamp parliament in the early years made his government "the most purely arbitrary in the world". The Constituent Law of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/corte">Cortes</a> of 1942 created a purely advisory Cortes Españolas, elected by neither direct nor universal suffrage, and ministers were appointed and dismissed by Franco alone. The referendum law of 1945, in which only heads of families could vote, was used in 1947 to revive the monarchy with Franco as regent for life, and in 1966 to approve a new organic law creating the modern office of Prime Minister. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a> was one of the most centralised countries in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a>: Franco abolished regional autonomy and the fueros of Guipuzcoa and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/biscay">Biscay</a>, classed as "traitor regions".</p><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/catholicism">Catholicism</a> was the state religion, and the Church controlled the schools; the Ministry of Education under José Ibáñez Martín purged thousands of teachers. Women lost the rights the Republic had given them and could not become judges, and from 1941 the Women's Protection Board confined tens of thousands of girls and young women in centres where they were routinely brutalised. Homosexuality was criminalised from the Penal Code of 1944, and the Press Law of 1938 imposed prior censorship.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="impact">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Global &amp; Regional Impact</h2>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Franco's nationalism promoted a Castilian-centric unitary national identity by repressing <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a>'s diversity. The government revoked the official status the Republic had given Basque, Galician and Catalan, forbade other languages in schools, documents, religious ceremonies and signs, and promoted bullfighting and flamenco as national traditions. For nearly twenty years the regime presented the Civil War as a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/crusade">crusade</a> against Bolshevism in defence of Christian civilisation, the central myth of a national rebirth. The silence imposed on families of the Republic was carried into the 1977 Pact of Forgetting.</p><p>Historians long debated its nature. Until 1964 it was commonly defined as totalitarian and fascist; <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/juan-jose-linz">Juan José Linz</a> then called it authoritarian, with "limited political pluralism", and Payne called its first phase "semi-fascist". Paul Preston and Julián Casanova later placed it in the family of European fascism, and Martin Blinkhorn calls it "the only European regime with a major radical fascist ingredient to survive long beyond 1945". The regime supported neo-fascist movements in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a> up to the 1960s and the "ratlines" for war criminals. The Argentine General Juan Carlos Onganía modelled his military regime of 1966–1970 on Francoist Spain, and Francoism influenced Chile's military <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dictatorship">dictatorship</a>.</p></div><div class="profile-inv"><p class="profile-inv-k">Songs in history</p><p class="profile-inv-l"><a href="/songs/marcha-real">Marcha Real<span>1761</span></a><a href="/songs/cara-al-sol">Cara al Sol<span>1935</span></a></p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="decline">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Decline &amp; Fall</h2>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Ties to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/axis-powers">Axis</a> brought isolation after 1945: <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a> was explicitly condemned in a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-nations">United Nations</a> resolution of 1946 and was not admitted until 1955. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cold-war">Cold War</a> changed this, as Franco's anti-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/communism">communism</a> tilted the regime towards the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a> in the early 1950s. On 26 July 1947 Spain was declared a kingdom with the throne left vacant; Franco passed over Infante Juan, Count of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/barcelona">Barcelona</a>, whom he found too liberal, and in 1969 named Juan Carlos of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/house-of-bourbon">Bourbon</a> his heir. The Press and Print Law of 1966 ended prior censorship but kept controls, and the restrictions on languages and on women eased somewhat in the 1960s and 1970s.</p><p>In 1973 Franco resigned as Prime Minister and named Admiral <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/luis-carrero-blanco">Luis Carrero Blanco</a>, who was meant to continue the regime, but ETA assassinated him the same year and Carlos Arias Navarro took his place. In 1975 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/morocco">Morocco</a> took control of the former Spanish territories in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sahara">Sahara</a> after the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/green-march">Green March</a>. Franco died on 20 November 1975, and Juan Carlos, now King, began the transition that made Spain a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/constitutional-monarchy">constitutional monarchy</a> with an elected parliament and devolved governments. The Historical Memory Law of 2007 sought official recognition of the regime's crimes against civilians and a state-supervised search for mass graves.</p></div>
      </div>

    <p class="profile-summary" id="summary"><em>Francoist <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a> was the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dictatorship">dictatorship</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/francisco-franco">Francisco Franco</a>, born in the Nationalist zone in 1936 and ruling all Spain from 1939 to 1975. Built on a single party, the Church and the army, it imprisoned and executed its opponents, repressed regional languages and women's rights, kept to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/axis-powers">Axis</a> side of neutrality in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-world-war">Second World War</a>, and after 1959 presided over rapid economic growth.</em></p>

